An embedding is a vector representation of data in embedding space.

An embedding is a numerical vector that represents some piece of data (like a word, image, user, or product) inside a continuous, multi‑dimensional space called “embedding space.”

“Embedding space” is the geometric space (often 100–1000 dimensions) where similar items end up close together and dissimilar items are farther apart.

One-hot vector
-every word (or category) is represented as a vector with a single “1” and all other entries “0.”
-distance between any 2 vectors is the same, regardless of the words. (nothing to do with the 'meaning' of the words)
-fine for indexing, but useless for capturing similarity or meaning. That’s why embeddings are introduced — they map words into a continuous vector space where distances reflect semantic closeness.

A vector space is a collection of vectors, and is characterized by its dimension.
The dot product acts as a similarity metric because it will tend to be high just when
the two vectors have large values in the same dimensions.

